            <td data-label="Core work">{row.work}</td>
            <td data-label="Pick this over veterinary if">{row.pickInstead}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    None of these beats veterinary outright, and veterinary does not beat them outright either. It wins on
    genuine, broad structural demand across livestock, dairy, poultry, and a fast-growing pet-care segment
    for someone who actually wants animal-facing clinical work. It loses to human allied health on speed to
    a first paycheque, and it should never be the fallback answer for someone whose real interest was human
    medicine. Curious how a medical-adjacent path without a clinical licence compares? See
    <a href="/blog/medical-careers/career-options-in-medical-field-other-than-doctor-india/">career options in the medical field other than doctor</a>
    for the allied-health side of this decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-fits">Who this career genuinely fits</h2>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Hands-on animal work does not unsettle you</div>
      <p>Restraining a frightened animal, handling blood and injury, and making a euthanasia call are regular parts of the job, not rare exceptions. Someone who can do this without it wearing them down is fitting the actual work, not the idea of it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">You are building a specific plan, not waiting for a job to appear</div>
      <p>With roughly three job-seekers for every formal opening in the field, the strongest outcomes belong to people who target a government exam, a clinical specialisation, or their own practice deliberately, not to people who assume the degree alone guarantees work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">You can work with farmers and pet owners equally well</div>
      <p>Whether your week runs on rural dispensary rounds or metro pet consultations, the job is as much about earning a stressed client's trust as it is about the medicine itself.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="who-pause">Who should think twice before committing</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Warning sign</th>
          <th>What is actually true</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>Choosing veterinary because your MBBS rank fell short</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">This is the single most common, most damaging reason students enter this field without real fit. Veterinary medicine has its own physical, emotional, and clinical demands that have nothing to do with human medicine, and students who arrive this way tend to struggle through the actual work.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>Assuming every vet ends up in a comfortable metro pet clinic</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Most of India's veterinary infrastructure and a large share of government postings are livestock-first, rural work. A student who only wants pet-clinic work should plan deliberately for it, not assume it is the default outcome.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>Paying management-quota private fees without a clear income plan</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Rs 12-25 lakh in total fees on a management-quota seat needs to be weighed honestly against entry pay bands of Rs 30,000-80,000 a month. Without a specific specialisation or ownership plan, that math takes years to work in your favour.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Recognising yourself in one of these rows does not automatically rule veterinary out. It means the
    reason behind the choice deserves a second, honest look before a NEET attempt and years of study go in.
    A different PCB-adjacent path, or simply a clearer plan inside veterinary itself, can fix the actual
    problem.
  </p>

